.TH FERROKEYD 1 "August 2026" "Ferrokey" "Ferrokey Manual"
.SH NAME
ferrokeyd \- the Ferrokey broker: supervisor, bootstrap, and sandboxed runtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ferrokeyd
.B start
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-foreground\fR]
.br
.B ferrokeyd
.B init
\fIhandoff-fd\fR [\fB\-\-device\-name\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-held\-keys\fR \fIn\fR]
.br
.B ferrokeyd
.B serve
\fIhandoff-fd\fR [options]
.br
.B ferrokeyd
.B sandbox-probe
[\fB\-\-session\-scope\fR \fIscope\fR]
.br
.B ferrokeyd
.B security-status
\fB\-\-pid\fR \fIpid\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ferrokeyd
owns the single pre-created
.I /dev/uinput
virtual keyboard and serves authenticated clients (the
.BR ferrokey (1)
UI) over an AF_UNIX socket. It is a constrained broker: after startup it
runs non-root with zero Linux capabilities,
.BR NO_NEW_PRIVS ,
a seccomp allowlist, an FD inventory, and rate-limited per-connection key
delivery.
.PP
The command is split into three lifecycle components plus two diagnostic
tools. The supervisor
.RB ( start )
parses the root-owned security-boundary configuration, spawns the bootstrap
.RB ( init ),
then spawns the runtime
.RB ( serve )
which drops privileges and freezes itself before serving any client.
.PP
.B ferrokeyd serve
refuses to run as root
.RB ( \-\-allow-root
is a development override that prints a warning and is never enabled
implicitly).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS start
.B ferrokeyd start
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-foreground\fR]
.PP
Run the supervisor: parse the root-owned config (see
.BR ferrokeyd.yaml (5)),
spawn
.B init
to create and transfer the device, then spawn
.B serve
with the dropped identity, and reap both children.
.TP
.B \-\-config \fIpath\fR
Security-boundary configuration. Must be owned by root and mode 0644 when
running privileged; a user-writable config is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Accepted for compatibility; the supervisor is always in the foreground.
.PP
Privilege expectations: root at startup (to open /dev/uinput, setuid/setgid
to the configured service identity, and load the root-owned config).
.PP
Exit conditions: success when the runtime broker reports ready; non-zero on
config error, device failure, or child failure.
.SS init
.B ferrokeyd init
\fIhandoff-fd\fR [\fB\-\-device\-name\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-held\-keys\fR \fIn\fR]
.PP
Bootstrap: open /dev/uinput, configure the exact immutable capability set,
create one virtual keyboard
.RB ( UI_DEV_CREATE ),
verify its identity and capability bitmap from sysfs, transfer the device
fd to the supervisor over SCM_RIGHTS on
.I handoff-fd ,
and exit.
.PP
Intended operational use: never run manually. The supervisor invokes it.
.SS serve
.B ferrokeyd serve
\fIhandoff-fd\fR [options]
.PP
Runtime broker: adopt the transferred device fd, bind the AF_UNIX socket,
drop supplementary groups then gid then uid to the configured service
identity, clear all capabilities, set
.BR NO_NEW_PRIVS ,
install the seccomp filter, verify the FD inventory, and then serve clients.
.PP
Options (mirroring the config fields; the config is the normal source):
.TP
.B \-\-socket \fIpath\fR
Socket path (default
.IR /run/ferrokeyd/ferrokeyd.sock ).
.TP
.B \-\-socket\-mode \fIoctal\fR
Socket mode applied after creation (default 0666).
.TP
.B \-\-max\-conn \fIn\fR
Maximum concurrent clients (default 1, hard limit 64).
.TP
.B \-\-max\-held \fIn\fR
Maximum simultaneously-held keys (default 16, hard limit 32).
.TP
.B \-\-burst \fIn\fR ", " \fB\-\-per\-sec\fR \fIn\fR
Token-bucket rate limits for client messages (defaults 200/200).
.TP
.B \-\-device\-name \fIname\fR
The virtual keyboard device name (default
.RB "Ferrokey Virtual Keyboard" ).
.TP
.B \-\-uid \fIlist\fR ", " \fB\-\-gid\fR \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated SO_PEERCRED whitelist (default: deny-all).
.TP
.B \-\-session\-scope \fIscope\fR
Optional logind session-scope binding (form
.IR session-N.scope );
clients outside the bound session scope are refused.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-root
Development/testing override: allow serving as root. Prints a warning.
.PP
Privilege expectations: the runtime must already be non-root with the
device fd in hand (the supervisor arranges this). Output: structured log
lines; on failure the serve child exits non-zero and the supervisor fails.
.SS sandbox-probe
.B ferrokeyd sandbox-probe
[\fB\-\-session\-scope\fR \fIscope\fR]
.PP
Host-safe diagnostic: install the exact runtime seccomp filter in this
process
.RB ( NO_NEW_PRIVS ,
filter, enforcement probes) and print the probe report. Exits non-zero if
any forbidden syscall is not denied. Used by the kernel-security court and
by operators to prove the filter that would be installed.
.TP
.B \-\-session\-scope \fIscope\fR
Exercise the session-gated filter variant (form
.IR session-N.scope ).
.SS security-status
.B ferrokeyd security-status
\fB\-\-pid\fR \fIpid\fR
.PP
Read-only diagnostics of a running broker: print the Uid/Gid, capability
sets, seccomp mode and NO_NEW_PRIVS of the given pid (typically the serve
process). Intended for verifying the freeze externally.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The security-boundary configuration is
.BR ferrokeyd.yaml (5),
normally at
.IR /etc/ferrokey/ferrokeyd.yaml .
It must be root-owned and not group/world-writable when the supervisor runs
privileged.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter for the runtime logs (e.g.
.RB info ,
.RB "ferrokeyd=trace" ).
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Used only by the UI; the broker socket path comes from the config.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ferrokey/ferrokeyd.yaml
Security-boundary configuration (root-owned, mode 0644).
.TP
.I /run/ferrokeyd/ferrokeyd.sock
Default broker socket.
.TP
.I /dev/uinput
The kernel interface the bootstrap opens.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, device, sandbox, or runtime failure. The serve child exits
non-zero if the freeze fails (fail-closed).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On startup the supervisor logs the freeze report (euid/egid, capability
sets, NO_NEW_PRIVS, seccomp, FD list) and the enforcement probe report.
.B sandbox-probe
prints the same probe report to stdout for offline proof.
.B security-status
prints the /proc status of the serve pid.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the broker from the packaged config:
.nf
    ferrokeyd start \-\-config /etc/ferrokey/ferrokeyd.yaml
.fi
Prove the sandbox that would be installed (with session binding):
.nf
    ferrokeyd sandbox-probe \-\-session\-scope session-2.scope
.fi
Verify a running broker's freeze externally:
.nf
    ferrokeyd security-status \-\-pid $(pgrep \-f 'proc/self/exe serve')
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ferrokeyd.yaml (5),
.BR ferrokey (1),
.BR ferrokey.yaml (5)
